        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="13" relname="span">Two design principles that I believe can significantly affect the intuitiveness of an interface are mapping and affordance (Preece et al, 2002).</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="1" relname="elaboration">Mapping with respect to a system interface is the relationship between controls and their effect on the system.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="13" relname="elaboration">According to Norman (2002), the movement, placement, and appearance of a control should give a visual clue as to what effect that control will have on the system.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="5" relname="condition">I believe that if an interface effectively meets the mapping principle,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="14" relname="span">it has a greater probability of being considered intuitive by the user.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="11" relname="elaboration">In addition, Preece et al define the principle of affordance as an attribute of an object that allows people to know how to use it.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="15" relname="span">It seems to me that the principle of affordance is closely related to the principle of mapping.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="elaboration">In any event, interfaces that are considered intuitive by their users have probably done a good job of meeting the design principles of mapping and affordance.</segment>
        <group id="9" type="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="span" parent="9" relname="span"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="10" relname="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="11" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="12" relname="span"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="12" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="10" relname="elaboration"/>
